=== MDV-P2-016
title: Allergy and interaction safety checks before authorisation
type: Story
epic: MDV-P2-E05
prd: P2-06
screens: E-11
covers: exit:2
priority: MUST
roles: Doctor
labels: safety, prescribing
estimate: 3
weeks: 13
depends: MDV-P2-015
confidence: 70 | Check logic is clear; interaction knowledge base source/licence is not yet chosen and must be clinically validated.
sources: PRD v2 P2-06, exit 2; docs/02-design/screen-specs.md
desc:
Checks run on every add/change and again on authorisation: allergy/cross-sensitivity (e.g. penicillin class), drug–drug interactions (severity levels), duplicate therapy, dose range per age/renal function where data exists (e.g. "Renal dose OK · eGFR 72"). Severe findings require an override reason; results show as chips with words, not colour only.
ac:
- A seeded conflict set (allergy class, interacting pairs, duplicates, out-of-range doses) triggers the expected alert in 100 % of cases (automated test; exit gate 2).
- Authorising with a severe alert requires an override reason, recorded and audited.
- Alerts state severity in words and the evidence (e.g. "Allergy: Penicillin · verified").
- The interaction source and version are shown in an info panel and recorded with each check result.

=== MDV-P2-017
title: Rx authorisation (separate from note), server-side multilingual printed Rx A4/A5 with hash and QR verify (E-10)
type: Story
epic: MDV-P2-E05
prd: P2-06
screens: E-10
covers: nfr:2, nfr:3, exit:4
priority: MUST
roles: Doctor
labels: prescribing, print, pdf
estimate: 3
weeks: 13-14
depends: MDV-P2-016, MDV-P1-007
confidence: 82 | Print design exists; QuestPDF licence tier to confirm (Q-05).
sources: docs/02-design/source/Medvya Clinician App (E-10 Printed prescription); PRD v2 P2 NFR 2–3, exit 4
desc:
"Authorise prescription" is a separate action from signing the note (step-up auth). Server-side PDF (QuestPDF) in A4 and A5: clinic header/logo, doctor name, qualifications, licence number, signature image, patient identifiers, generic names first then brand, sig in English (a patient-language line later if pilots ask), scheduled-drug notes, QR code to verify authenticity; stored with SHA-256 hash.
ac:
- An Rx cannot be printed or shared until authorised; authorisation is audited.
- PDF renders server-side for A4 and A5 and is stored with a hash; the QR verify page shows validity without exposing PHI beyond what the patient holds.
- The PDF prints instructions in English; names and free text in Hindi, Tamil or Arabic print shaped (owner decision 11 Nov 2026: in India the pharmacist explains the medicines in the patient's language; a reviewed patient-language instruction line comes later if pilots ask for it).
- Each pilot doctor reviews and approves their own Rx layout (approval recorded per doctor; exit gate 4).
- Print layout passes 200 % zoom and screen-reader labelling checks in the on-screen preview.
- Given a prescription is printed, when it renders, then it shows the doctor's licence number and authority (moved from MDV-P1-065; the number and authority are already stored on DoctorLicence and attested by the owner).

